SLIs|A service-level indicator measures a user-relevant aspect of service behavior, such as successful checkouts or response latency.|Define the eligible population, good-event condition and measurement location. Successful events divided by eligible events gives an availability-style SLI. Excluding difficult requests or measuring only internal components can hide real user failures.|
SLOs|A service-level objective sets a target for an SLI over an explicit evaluation window.|Choose a target from user needs and the cost of reliability. For example, 99.9% successful eligible requests over 30 days leaves 0.1% unsuccessful requests. Specify low-traffic and missing-data behavior before using it for decisions.|
Error budgets|An error budget is the unreliability allowed by an SLO.|For an event-based SLO, allowed bad events equal eligible events multiplied by one minus the target. A shared budget policy determines when to prioritize stabilization, slow risky releases or accept experiments. Unused budget is not a reason to ignore incidents.|
Incident roles|Incident roles separate coordination, technical investigation and communication during an outage.|An incident commander prioritizes mitigation and assigns ownership; responders investigate bounded hypotheses; a communicator maintains updates. A shared timeline prevents conflicting changes and preserves decisions. Roles may be combined for small incidents but ownership should stay explicit.|
Mitigation|Mitigation reduces current customer impact before the full cause is understood.|A known-safe rollback, traffic shift or feature disablement can restore service faster than a code-level diagnosis. Evaluate reversibility, data compatibility and capacity at the destination. Continue collecting evidence without delaying an effective recovery action.|
Postmortems|A postmortem records impact, timeline, contributing conditions and improvements after an incident.|Blameless analysis examines why decisions made sense with the available information. Separate evidence from hypotheses, identify detection and recovery gaps, and assign concrete actions with owners and completion checks. A report without follow-through does not reduce recurrence.|
Toil|Toil is repetitive operational work that is automatable, reactive and tends to grow with service scale.|Measure frequency, duration and failure risk before selecting automation. Reducing the underlying need may be better than scripting every step. Valuable manual judgment and learning are not automatically toil.|
Runbooks|A runbook is a tested operational procedure for a recognizable condition.|Include prerequisites, symptoms, evidence, decision branches, bounded actions and escalation criteria. A useful runbook tells responders when to stop as well as what to run. Assign an owner and test it against changed systems.|
Burn rate|Burn rate compares observed bad-event rate with the rate allowed by the SLO.|For a 99.9% target, a 1% bad-event rate burns the budget at 10 times the sustainable rate. Combine long and short windows to detect sustained impact promptly without paging on every transient spike.|
Latency indicators|Latency indicators describe how long eligible user operations take or how often they meet a threshold.|A mean can hide slow tail requests. Use a threshold-based good-event ratio for an SLO and percentiles for diagnosis. Define whether failed, cancelled and timed-out requests contribute so the indicator cannot improve when users fail faster.|
Alert actionability|An actionable alert identifies a condition requiring a specific, timely human response.|Page on user impact or imminent exhaustion with ownership, context and a runbook. Route nonurgent work to tickets. Assess whether a responder could take a useful action; a fluctuating metric alone is not a paging requirement.|
Capacity headroom|Capacity headroom is usable spare capacity relative to expected demand and failure conditions.|Measure the limiting resource under representative workload, including dependencies and scaling delay. Reserve enough for a planned failure domain and forecast growth. Aggregate CPU headroom does not guarantee database or connection-pool headroom.|
Load shedding|Load shedding intentionally rejects lower-priority or excess work to keep essential work healthy.|Reject early before expensive processing, use bounded concurrency and communicate retry behavior. Evaluate fairness and business priority. Returning controlled failures can preserve overall usefulness better than allowing every request to time out.|
Backpressure|Backpressure communicates downstream saturation so upstream producers reduce work.|Bounded queues, concurrency limits and explicit rejection can propagate pressure. If upstream ignores the signal or retries aggressively, overload grows. Measure queue depth and oldest-item age to distinguish a short burst from sustained capacity shortage.|
Retry storms|A retry storm occurs when repeated attempts amplify traffic to a failing dependency.|Retries across several layers multiply, especially when clients synchronize. Limit attempts and concurrent retries, add exponential backoff with jitter, and honor end-to-end deadlines. Recovery may require reducing retry traffic before scaling the dependency.|
Recovery testing|Recovery testing demonstrates that backups, failover and runbooks can restore a usable service.|Exercise dependencies, credentials, DNS, data integrity and user transactions in an isolated or controlled environment. Record actual recovery time and recovered data age. A successful backup job is evidence of creation, not proof of restoration.|
RTO|Recovery time objective is the target maximum time to restore the required service capability after disruption.|Define when the clock starts and what counts as restored. Detection, decision-making, provisioning and validation all consume time. Measure complete recovery exercises against the target rather than timing only a database restore command.|
RPO|Recovery point objective is the target maximum data loss expressed as time.|Backup interval and replication lag influence the recoverable point, but consistency and accessibility also matter. Verify the actual recovered transactions. Synchronous replication can reduce some loss windows but does not protect against every logical deletion or corruption.|
Canary releases|A canary release exposes a bounded portion of traffic to a new version before wider rollout.|Compare representative cohorts with predefined success and abort criteria. Small samples and traffic differences can mislead. Automate stopping or rollback only when the measurement and rollback path are trustworthy.|
Feature flags|Feature flags change behavior without requiring a new deployment.|They enable gradual exposure and rapid disabling of risky paths. Flag services and defaults become dependencies, so define behavior during their failure. Remove obsolete flags to avoid untested combinations and permanent complexity.|
Dependencies|A dependency is a service or resource required for an operation's success.|Map critical and optional dependencies, their timeout budgets and failure modes. Protect shared dependencies with isolation, caching or bounded fallback where semantics allow. A healthy frontend cannot compensate for an unavailable mandatory write path.|
Graceful degradation|Graceful degradation preserves essential functions while reducing optional functionality under failure.|For example, checkout may remain available while recommendations are disabled. Fallbacks need explicit correctness, freshness and capacity limits. Returning stale or incomplete data is acceptable only when the product's contract permits it.|
Chaos experiments|A chaos experiment tests a specific resilience hypothesis by introducing a controlled failure.|Define the expected steady state, scope, observability and abort condition before injecting a fault. Start with a small blast radius and a working recovery path. Randomly breaking production without a hypothesis is not a useful experiment.|
Incident communication|Incident communication gives stakeholders timely, accurate information about impact and recovery.|State what is known, what remains uncertain, current mitigation and the next update time. Avoid speculative root causes or unsupported restoration estimates. Keep technical coordination and customer-facing updates consistent without exposing unnecessary sensitive detail.|
SLO aggregation|SLO aggregation combines measurements across populations or services.|Weight event-based ratios by their eligible event counts; averaging percentages gives small populations disproportionate influence. Also inspect slices such as region or tenant because a global average can conceal a severe localized failure.|
Overload recovery|Overload recovery restores a system whose demand exceeds its effective capacity.|First reduce incoming work, retries or backlog processing so recovery has room to succeed. Ramp traffic gradually and watch queues and dependency latency. Restarting every instance simultaneously can destroy caches and make overload worse.|
Consistency trade-offs|Consistency describes which observations of distributed state clients can rely on.|Replication and failures create choices involving staleness, availability and coordination latency. State the consistency needed for each operation instead of labeling the whole system with one slogan. Money movement and recommendations often have different requirements.|
Reliability investment|Reliability investment allocates engineering effort to reduce expected user impact and operational cost.|Compare incident frequency, severity, recovery difficulty and implementation cost. Use SLO performance and business needs to prioritize work. More redundancy can introduce complexity, so verify that it addresses the actual failure mode.|
Incident metrics|Incident metrics describe detection, response, recovery and customer impact across events.|Use consistent start and end definitions and examine distributions, severity and sample size. A lower average recovery time can mask a few catastrophic incidents. Metrics should support learning rather than incentivize reclassification or premature closure.|
Safe automation|Safe automation performs repeatable operations within explicit preconditions and boundaries.|Validate targets, use least privilege, bound concurrency and make retries idempotent. Include dry-run or preview, stop conditions and observable results. Automation should fail clearly when assumptions change instead of continuing across an entire fleet.|
